.TH CCOL_BEGIN 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
ccol_begin \- begin iteration over any supported container
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/citerators.h>   /* or any container header */

cmap_iterator *ccol_begin(container);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
ccol_begin returns an iterator on the first element of container. The
begin_iter function to call is picked at compile time with _Generic on
the type of container. The supported types are cvec, chmap and cbmap, and
a container of any other type (a void * included) is a compile error.

An empty container gives NULL and no error. A NULL container handle
iterates as an empty container, so it gives NULL and no error as well. If
an allocation fails, the macro calls _ccol_fatal_err().
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
container  A cvec, chmap or cbmap variable.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
A cmap_iterator* on the first element, or NULL when the container is
empty.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Declare the iterator with ccol_iter_declare(container, it) before you
call ccol_begin in a for loop, since that declaration is what gives you
typed accessors for it.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
ccol_iter_declare(vec, it);
for (it = ccol_begin(vec); it != NULL; it = ccol_iter_next(it)) {
    printf("[%zu] = %d\en", *ccol_iter_key_ptr(it),
                            *ccol_iter_val_ptr(it));
}
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_iter_declare (3),
.BR ccol_iter_next (3),
.BR ccol_for_each (3),
.BR ccol_end (3)
